From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: I**2: "innate" intelligence

>Tim May writes:
>>we can't alter our own innate intelligence
>
>Can you give an operational account of the meaning of the word "innate" in
>this claim, and at least pointers to evidence of its truth?
>
>--                                ____
>Richard Kennaway                  \ _/__    School of Information Systems

I mean that I've seen no reliable evidence that intelligence per se (as
opposed to test-taking skills or specific knowledge) can be changed
significantly past some age. By the time people are reading this list (18
years old or more, typically, and perhaps as old as 30s or 40s), basic
intelligence is not easily changeable.

This doesn't mean it *can't* be changed, just that a bottle of pills from
the back of "Omni" magazine, or a test put out by some hucksters, is going
to do it.

And I don't even know if prescription-only drugs will do it, either (that
is, more powerful drugs). I've heard no reliable reports of true
intelligence-enhancing drugs.

Someone on this list (could it have been Charlie Stross?) speculated
several months back that simple intelligence enhancing drugs are fairly
unlikely, because of the complexity of what intelligence is. If a simple
chemical could enhance intelligence, evolution would likely have
"discovered" this chemical (assuming, as is likely, that an IQ boost of 10
or 20 points would have differential reproduction value).

-Tim
