Date: Mon, 5 Apr 93 12:47:14 PDT
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: I**2: "innate" intelligence

Tim May said:
> Someone on this list (could it have been Charlie Stross?) speculated
> several months back that simple intelligence enhancing drugs are fairly
> unlikely, because of the complexity of what intelligence is. If a simple
> chemical could enhance intelligence, evolution would likely have
> "discovered" this chemical

Evolution is just not that efficient, particularly when it has less
than a few billion years to work with.  Intelligence has only been a
significant factor in human evolution for a million years or so, and
a major but unspectacular hereditable increase might not be a big
reproductive win until the development of language, maybe even of
written language.  This does not leave enough time to do anything.

In fact, there are counterexamples of varying relevance which have
had a lot more CPU for their GA runs.  The immune system is a very
complex thing, but it would have worked far better if it had known
how to synthesize penicillin when necessary.  Fungi discovered it;
we didn't.  There's plenty of room for productive hackery on
even evolution's best designs.

That said, increasing intelligence is still a harder task than
killing bacteria.  But I see no reason why it should be impossible.

