Date: Mon, 5 Apr 93 16:56:17 EDT
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: I**2: "innate" intelligence

It wouldn't surprise me if there are simple methods of increasing
"intelligence".  Hell, studying will help!  But evolution has no
interest in solving the problem of maximizing intelligence, just as it
has no interest in solving the problem of maximum longevity.
Evolution tries to solve the far more interesting problem of
increasing intelligence without incurring a penalty which is more
costly than the benefit of increased intelligence.

That sounds like vaporware, but I've seen it stated that (1)
intelligence is strongly correlated with nerve conduction speed (CPU
cycles/sec!), and (2) the human brain consumes 10% of the resting
metabolic demand.  It may be that jacking up the cycle rate will make
us smarter, but we may not want to pay the power for it!  (Of course,
much of the population isn't subject to periodic famine anymore.)

But really, you can't change innate intelligence, simply because
"innate" means *what you can't change*.

Dale

