If we define intelligence as the ability to quickly obtain new
knowledge, we must consider external factors such as scholarly
research technology.  At least one "intelligence" test, The MegaBrain 
test allows research.  Practical problem solving also heavily involves
research.  During the last decade we've seen a boom in research 
technologies: on-line catalogs, abstracts, & journals, e-mail & news, 
faxes, etc.  

I know these have had a big impact on my "intelligence" as defined here.
I went into a podunk public library yesterday and read the recent Forbes 
article on Digital Capitalism (_highly_ recommended, BTW).  There I found 
one Richard Sandor is developing futures that could revolutionize the
insurance market, which ties in with market applications of Robin Hanson's
idea futures that I have been considering.  I immediately walked over
to the CD-ROM and searched for "Richard Sandor" in the Wall St. Journal
1990-1993, and found several nifty articles discussing pollution rights
and futures, insurance futures, etc.  I read the Wall St. Journal 
regularly but I had missed those articles.  In five minutes using
a computer I had accomplished what would have taken many hours paging
through the newspaper (practically speaking, I wouldn't have even
tried).

I wonder how long the MegaBrain test been around, and how long have 
scores been kept?  I suspect that scores may have increased over the
last decade, just due to the introduction of tech like on-line
literature searches.

> If a simple
> chemical could enhance intelligence, evolution would likely have
> "discovered" this chemical (assuming, as is likely, that an IQ boost of 10
> or 20 points would have differential reproduction value).

I don't buy this.  Getting rid of the overgeneralized term
"intelligence" for the moment, the kind of thinking that is
important for extropy in today's society (scholarly research,
mathematical ability, logical & probabilistic reasoning, etc.)
is quite different than the kind of thinking important to genetic
fitness in a hunter-gatherer society (where are the game,
can I seduce the neighbor's wife without getting hit over the head, 
etc.)  Any chemical that, for example, eliminates the distractions
of gratuitous sex and appetite would automatically increase my practical 
daily ability to gather and use new information.  It may also be
possible to reallocate resources (energy, nutrients, etc.) from one
function to another with chemicals.

I find huge misallocations in my own intelligence.  For example, why
is it that I can remember 100's of tunes, probably several Kbytes
apiece of data, often after only hearing the tune once or twice, but
I'm so bad at remembering a few bytes worth of phone numbers?
If there was some way to reroute the circuits of the muse to more 
practical applications, I might be able to massively improve my
skills.  It would also be nice to remember text with the same 
thruput I with which I can recognize faces, etc.  Now, most of
these improvements probably require much more than a "simple chemical",
but it is concievable that certain chemicals can bias the functionality
in beneficial ways.

It should also be noted that our metabolisms depend on nutrients that
are often in short supply in the environment, eg vitamins and minerals.
Just as malnourished children (== normally nourished in evolutionary
timescales) suffer from IQ deficits, it could be that those malnourished
wrt high doses of certain nutrients or artificial substances 
may also suffer from IQ deficits.

