Dale Worley said:
> That sounds like vaporware, but I've seen it stated that (1)
> intelligence is strongly correlated with nerve conduction speed (CPU
> cycles/sec!), 

This sounds a little peculiar, just because I don't think nerve
conduction speed varies much between individuals.  Different
proportions of myelinated cells, maybe.

> and (2) the human brain consumes 10% of the resting
> metabolic demand.

But this sounds about right.  A large fraction of total energy is
spent on active transport, and maintaining ion gradients in the
brain is probably a good fraction of that.

Whether or not intelligence is correlated with conduction speed,
you're not going to be able to increase intelligence, or "speed of
thinking", just by jacking up conduction speed.  It would really
screw things up, most likely.  Brain microcircuits which rely on
transmission delays for things like resonant loops and feedback
pulse shaping, both of which appear to happen in mammalian brains,
would be very confused.  If transmission delay along the dendritic
tree is used for motion detection, which is a bit more dubious,
you'd end up with permanent `trailers' to boot.

