admit that a single number isn't useful for doing so.  I'm not so
concerned with how I enhance my intelligence as I am with the benefits
of doing so.  Of course a more "portable" method is prefered but I am
willing to use "artificial" methods when necessary.

Known ways to "artificially" enhance individual intelligence:
books, computers, paper & pencil, white boards, ask someone else, ...

We are all familiar with the task of "getting on" to a problem.  This involves
selecting the "right" tool.  Is it Bayesian, a Markov Process, Chaos,
fuzzy, Zen, discrete event simulation, brute force, ...?

So the real issue is having the necessary IQ^H^H Brain Utility to "get
on" to the problem in the first place.  And sometimes you must invent
the tool itself.  These are the Newton's, Einsteins, etc. that were talked
about earlier.

