Subject: ECON: Conservation's externality

   From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>

   No, silly, he's talking about a positive externality.  But I don't see how
   this externality is different from that of the market-in-general itself -- I
   benefit from the existence of the idea of Vietnamese food, arguably more
   than from the existence of particular Vietnamese restaurants in my city, but
   I don't imagine anyone suggesting that the V-food meme ought to be protected
   by monopoly.

   The patent system is meant to encourage the production of ideas, not to
   assure their preservation.

The problem with preserving a rare species is for the preserver to
obtain ownership of the species, and thus, presumably, the derivatives
of that species that make maintaining its existance valuable.  Various
problems arise:

1) The preserver has to obtain ownership of *all* members of the
species, or he doesn't have a monopoly on it, and loses much of the
value of his ownership.  One can envision the owner of the rights
to a species going around and killing all the members that he doesn't
own, in order to maintain his monopoly...

2) The "valuable derivatives" of most species are future predictions,
not present realities.  Thus, conservation is a long-term, highly
risky investment, which is difficult to do effectively.  Perhaps the
investment should be organized into a mutual fund, which owns a
portfolio of species.  I assume that the mutual fund would concentrate
in species that live in a particular ecosystem, in order to take
advantage of the synergy of having to buy only one lot of land.

Dale

