   From: cappello%cs@hub.ucsb.edu (Peter Cappello)

   The attitude that I exhibit above is results-orientation.  I don't  
   want people to think that they own me (e.g., telling how to dress,  
   and how to behave, unless that is an inherent part of the task at  
   hand).  I want them to tell me what they want done.  People who are  
   competent tend to be results-oriented.  People who aren't tend to  
   make up for it with a slap on the back and a good joke.  I imagine  
   that most libertarians are more reliable, trustworthy, competent, and  
   results-oriented than average.  

The difficulty is that there are many situations where exactly what
the buyer wants done cannot be clearly specified in a contract.

If one wants an object carried from point A to point B, that can be
specified pretty clearly.

If one wants program debugging, it gets much more difficult to
specify, because it's hard to tell how much work the job will take
up-front.  So the payment will be set in dollars-per-hour.  But that
requires that the buyer trust that the seller will work "reasonably
hard" and is "reasonably good" at the job.  In a long-term
relationship (i.e., traditional employment), there is enough history
to be able to judge this up-front, but in a short-term one, it is very
hard.  That is one reason that judgment of sociological factors
(backslapping, wearing the correct suit, ad nauseum) is used -- a guy
who comes into a bank wearing a very conservative suit understands
bankers well enough to know what sort of suit is considered "proper",
so bankers trust him more -- there is known common ground between
them.

As a stronger example of non-contractible agreements, consider
managment consulting.  There, the consultant is brought in to see if
he can figure out what's going wrong and determine solutions, if
possible.  It's absolutely impossible to contract for results, since
it might not be possible to generate any results at all.  So the
actual agreement has to be based almost entirely on reputation and the
trust involved in long-term relationships.

   We like rules _less_ than most people.  Perhaps you are confusing a  
   quest for clear understanding with a quest for rules.  Please don't.

I must not be explaining myself clearly...  By "rules", I *mean* a
clear understanding of the sort that can be unambiguously judged by an
external observer.  Whether the rules are voluntarily acceeded to or
imposed by an authority is not particularly relevant.  Rules
can be enforced by a court system, using adversarial judgment,
precisely because their import is clear.

Contrasting, I call "relationship" a situation where the players base
their interaction upon mutual interest, due to mutual understanding
(long history of interaction) and a belief that the reltionship will
continue for a long time into the future.  A relationship can be used
successfully in situations in which rules just don't work.  I gave
several examples above, but the primary case is family relationships:
We talk about a "marriage contract", but the real agreement of
marriage is a relationship, not rules.  It's very hard to imagine how
to structure a true marriage contract that actually captures what one
expects out of marriage.  An even stronger case is the parent-child
relationship, where one party isn't even able to negotiate the
contract...  (A relationship is the result of an Iterated Prisoner's
Dilemma.)

Governments can operate either by rules (e.g., the US) or by
relationships (e.g., Japan).  Rule-based systems result in lots of
litigation, but a higher degree of flexibility, since relationships
are (of necessity) based on long-term interaction between the players.

   Would you say, then, that a person who asks you to sacrifice your  
   life for the "relationship" is goodwilled?  In that case, would you  
   say that a person is badwilled, if they ask you not to sacrifice your  
   life for the relationship?

   Goodwill != self-sacrifice.  True goodwill and self-interest are  
   largely in accord.  Pursuit of a quick buck has nothing to do with  
   either of them.  

Goodwill is that property of a person which makes them willing to make
sacrifices "for the relationship".  It's what makes it possible for
the other parties to depend that he will not exploit a short-term
advantage to reap a large profit and then go elsewhere.  Pursuit of a
quick buck is what poisons relationships, and since it is often
profitable to do so ("There's one born every minute."), trying to keep
quick-buck-ism suppressed is very difficult.

   We agree that true goodwill cannot be ensured through legalistic  
   mechanisms.

Yes.  The trick is to figure out how it *can* be ensured.

   From: KMOSTA01@ulkyvx.louisville.edu

   The statement is blatantly false, libertarians in fact argue for
   removal of almost all "rules", while accepting "relationships".

Sorry, I didn't explain myself well.  (See above.)  As far as I can
tell, libertarians generally like the idea of situations that are
governed by well-defined contracts, which is what I mean by "rules".
In fact, libertarians seem to actively seek out or construct such
situations.  Nowhere else have I seen an *explicit* market in
reputations.  Nowhere else have I seen a mailing list with a set of
official rules, with a semi-formal mechanism for adjudicating
violations.

Maybe I'm seeing a non-representative sample of life, but libertarians
generally seem to have a greater taste for unambiguous, rule-based
intereaction than the average member of the public.

Dale

